From: OpenHarmony PC team
Subject: [PATCH] efi: keep the legacy (GRUB owned) shim_lock kernel loader

Upstream GRUB 2.14 falls back to grub_cmd_linux_x86_legacy() - i.e. GRUB
loads the kernel itself through the EFI handover protocol - whenever the
legacy shim_lock verifier protocol is in use.  The firmware's LoadImage is
then never involved, so the kernel only has to be trusted by shim (MOK),
not by the firmware db.

The Debian/Ubuntu package removes that fallback
(debian/patches/secure-boot/disable-efi-fallback-to-legacy.patch).  With
shim 15.8 (which implements only shim_lock, not SHIM_IMAGE_LOADER_GUID)
the distribution GRUB therefore asks the firmware to load the kernel, the
firmware only trusts db, and the boot fails with

    error: cannot load image.

We build from pristine upstream, so the fallback is present.  This patch
keeps it reachable and adds an explicit opt-in knob
(ohos_force_legacy_loader=1) that forces the GRUB owned loader even with a
shim that implements SHIM_IMAGE_LOADER_GUID.

Signed-off-by: OpenHarmony PC team
---
--- a/grub-core/loader/efi/linux.c
+++ b/grub-core/loader/efi/linux.c
@@ -18,6 +18,7 @@
 
 #include <grub/charset.h>
 #include <grub/command.h>
+#include <grub/env.h>
 #include <grub/err.h>
 #include <grub/file.h>
 #include <grub/fdt.h>
@@ -483,6 +484,50 @@
 
   grub_dl_ref (my_mod);
 
+  /* OHOS: keep the legacy (GRUB owned) x86 kernel loader reachable.
+   *
+   * Upstream GRUB loads the kernel through the firmware (grub_efi_linux_boot
+   * -> EFI LoadImage) unless the legacy shim_lock protocol is in use, in which
+   * case it falls back to grub_cmd_linux_x86_legacy(), i.e. GRUB loads the
+   * kernel itself (EFI handover) and the firmware never verifies it again.
+   *
+   * That fallback is what makes a MOK-only Secure Boot setup work: the kernel
+   * file is verified by shim through the shim_lock verifier (MokListRT), and
+   * because GRUB - not the firmware - performs the load, the firmware's "db"
+   * never has to know our certificate.
+   *
+   * The Debian/Ubuntu build of GRUB 2.14 removes this fallback
+   * (debian/patches/secure-boot/disable-efi-fallback-to-legacy.patch), which
+   * is why the distribution GRUB fails with "error: cannot load image." on a
+   * MOK-only machine.  We build from pristine upstream, so the fallback is
+   * there; the ohos_force_legacy_loader knob below additionally allows
+   * forcing the GRUB owned loader even when shim implements the newer
+   * SHIM_IMAGE_LOADER_GUID protocol (shim >= 16).
+   */
+  {
+    const char *force_legacy = grub_env_get ("ohos_force_legacy_loader");
+    int forced = (force_legacy != NULL
+		  && (force_legacy[0] == '1' || force_legacy[0] == 'y'
+		      || force_legacy[0] == 'Y'));
+
+#if defined(__i386__) || defined(__x86_64__)
+    if (forced && grub_is_using_legacy_shim_lock_protocol () != true)
+      {
+	grub_dprintf ("linux", "ohos_force_legacy_loader=1: using the legacy x86 kernel loader\n");
+
+	err = grub_cmd_linux_x86_legacy (cmd, argc, argv);
+
+	if (err == GRUB_ERR_NONE)
+	  return GRUB_ERR_NONE;
+	else
+	  goto fail;
+      }
+#else
+    if (forced)
+      grub_dprintf ("linux", "ohos_force_legacy_loader=1: ignored, not an x86 build\n");
+#endif
+  }
+
   if (grub_is_using_legacy_shim_lock_protocol () == true)
     {
 #if defined(__i386__) || defined(__x86_64__)
